# {{ name }}

The {{ name }} website, a **Corex client site**.

- **App code:** `{{ plugin_slug }}/` (`{{ namespace }}\`)
- **Theme:** `{{ theme_slug }}/`, a child of the CoreX theme
- **Specs / docs:** `specs/`, `docs/`
- **Workflow + agent rules:** [AGENTS.md](./AGENTS.md)
- **Taking a CoreX release:** [UPDATING-COREX.md](./UPDATING-COREX.md). The framework release
  this site is on is recorded in `corex-baseline.json`.

Edit only the client plugin/theme, never the Corex framework. Scaffold with `wp corex make:*`.

## The theme

`{{ theme_slug }}/` is a child of the CoreX theme (`Template: corex`). It inherits the CoreX theme's
templates, header and footer parts, patterns, style variations, block styles and fonts, so the
site looks finished before it has a file of its own.

- **Values** go in `{{ theme_slug }}/theme.json`. It lists every CoreX token with CoreX's value at
  the time the site was generated: change the values to the client's.
- **Structure** is overridden by a file of the same name: a `templates/front-page.html` or
  `parts/header.html` in `{{ theme_slug }}/` replaces the CoreX theme's, and a pattern in its
  `patterns/` replaces the CoreX pattern with the same `Slug:`.
  Create one when the client's design calls for it.
- **A list you keep must keep every entry.** A preset list in `theme.json` (the palette, the font
  families, the font sizes, the spacing sizes, the shadows) replaces the CoreX theme's whole list,
  so removing an entry removes that token from the site. `npm run verify:client-theme` checks it.
- **Only the client's patterns and templates in the editor:** set
  `settings.custom.corex.parent.hidePatterns` and `hideTemplates` to `true` in
  `{{ theme_slug }}/theme.json` (neither is set by default); pages still render with CoreX's templates.

The rules in full: `docs/en/04-team-workflow/client-site-workflow.md` in the framework.
